import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0066: Dependency-Graph Chunking","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0066-dependency-graph-chunking.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0066-dependency-graph-chunking.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0066-dependency-graph-chunking.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0066-dependency-graph-chunking" tabindex="-1">ADR-0066: Dependency-Graph Chunking <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0066-dependency-graph-chunking" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0066: Dependency-Graph Chunking”">​</a></h1><h2 id="status" tabindex="-1">Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#status" aria-label="Permalink to “Status”">​</a></h2><p>Proposed — deferred until a concrete driver exists.</p><p>Depends on <a href="./0063-scheme-core-completeness.html">ADR-0063 — Scheme-Core Completeness</a>, <a href="./0064-computational-language-authoring.html">ADR-0064 — Computational Language Authoring</a>, and <a href="./0065-derived-artifact-cache-and-compression.html">ADR-0065 — Derived-Artifact Cache and Compression</a>. Refines <a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044 — Language as Data and Runtime Split</a> and <a href="./0052-arrow-calculus-kernel.html">ADR-0052 — Arrow Calculus Kernel</a>.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><h3 id="the-insight-arrow-calculus-gives-a-real-dependency-graph" tabindex="-1">The insight: arrow calculus gives a real dependency graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-insight-arrow-calculus-gives-a-real-dependency-graph" aria-label="Permalink to “The insight: arrow calculus gives a real dependency graph”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0052 made the kernel arrow calculus: <code>arr</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>/<code>first</code>/<code>choice</code>/<code>loop</code>/<code>app</code>. The <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> combinator composes descriptions without running them (ADR-0052, README &quot;Descriptions, not execution&quot;). This means the compute graph is <strong>statically recoverable</strong> — you know the dependencies before you run anything. This is the property arrows have that monads don&#39;t (ADR-0041 §&quot;The arrow calculus solves the unpredictable-compute-graph problem&quot;), and it is what makes dependency-aware chunking precise rather than approximated.</p><p>Lambda-calculus languages approximate dependencies via control-flow analysis or escape analysis. Arrow-calculus languages <strong>read them off the <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> structure</strong>. This is the foundation for the chunking technique in this ADR.</p><h3 id="the-technique-regions-and-chunks-borrowed-from-game-optimization" tabindex="-1">The technique: regions and chunks, borrowed from game optimization <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-technique-regions-and-chunks-borrowed-from-game-optimization" aria-label="Permalink to “The technique: regions and chunks, borrowed from game optimization”">​</a></h3><p>Game engines partition worlds into regions and chunks for streaming: load only the chunks near the player, decompress on demand, unload when distant. The technique works because the dependency graph (what&#39;s in a chunk, what a chunk needs from neighbors) is known.</p><p>Applied to <code>Language</code> data:</p><ul><li>A <strong>region</strong> is a cluster of fragments with high internal dependency and low cross-region dependency (e.g., &quot;arithmetic operators,&quot; &quot;statement forms,&quot; &quot;type definitions for the standard library&quot;).</li><li>A <strong>chunk</strong> is a serializable, compressible unit within a region. A chunk carries its dependencies (references to other chunks, per ADR-0044 §7 <code>requires</code> and the <code>replaces</code> override graph).</li><li>The runtime loads chunks on demand: load the entry-point chunk, pull in its dependencies, typecheck the loaded subset, eval. Unloaded chunks are not in memory.</li></ul><h3 id="why-this-is-deferred" tabindex="-1">Why this is deferred <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-is-deferred" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this is deferred”">​</a></h3><p>The canonical <code>Language</code> is ~180KB. Chunking infrastructure (region partitioning, chunk serialization, lazy load, on-demand typecheck, reference resolution) has non-trivial overhead. At 180KB with one canonical kit, chunking likely costs more than it saves in memory, I/O, and startup time.</p><p>Chunking pays off when:</p><ul><li><strong>Multiple large kits</strong> are loaded in one process (each kit is a region; load only the active kit&#39;s chunks).</li><li><strong>Embedded/constrained targets</strong> with limited memory (load only what&#39;s needed for the current program).</li><li><strong>Network-loaded languages</strong> (stream chunks as needed, not the whole <code>Language</code>).</li><li><strong>Large language definitions</strong> (e.g., a <code>Language</code> with a rich standard library whose type definitions alone are megabytes).</li></ul><p>None of these drivers exist today. This ADR records the design — because the arrow-DAG chunking model is non-obvious and arrow-specific — but marks it <strong>deferred until a concrete driver appears</strong>. Building it speculatively is premature optimization.</p><h3 id="what-adr-0044-already-provides" tabindex="-1">What ADR-0044 already provides <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-adr-0044-already-provides" aria-label="Permalink to “What ADR-0044 already provides”">​</a></h3><p>ADR-0044 §7 specifies fragment-level <code>requires</code> (validated at merge time) and ADR-0044 §5 specifies the <code>replaces</code> override graph. These are the dependency edges chunking uses. The chunking layer does not invent a new dependency model; it partitions the existing one.</p><p>ADR-0044 §7 &quot;Compilers erase data dispatch&quot; notes that bytecode and cranelift engines compile away per-node scheme lookup. Chunking is orthogonal: it partitions the <em>data</em> (the <code>Language</code> value); the engines still compile whatever subset is loaded.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-regions-are-computed-from-the-fragment-dependency-graph" tabindex="-1">1. Regions are computed from the fragment dependency graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-regions-are-computed-from-the-fragment-dependency-graph" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Regions are computed from the fragment dependency graph”">​</a></h3><p>A region is a maximal set of fragments where every fragment&#39;s <code>requires</code> (ADR-0044 §7) and <code>replaces</code> (ADR-0044 §5) targets are within the set, or in a declared dependency region. Region partitioning is computed from the fragment graph, not hand-assigned:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>region_partition(fragments) -&gt; Vec&lt;Region&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  where Region = { fragments: FragmentSet, dep_regions: Set&lt;RegionId&gt; }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The partition algorithm is a graph clustering (e.g., Tarjan&#39;s strongly connected components for the <code>requires</code>/<code>replaces</code> graph, then merge small SCCs into regions by a size target). The result is deterministic given the same fragment set.</p><h3 id="_2-chunks-are-serializable-compressible-units-within-a-region" tabindex="-1">2. Chunks are serializable, compressible units within a region <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-chunks-are-serializable-compressible-units-within-a-region" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Chunks are serializable, compressible units within a region”">​</a></h3><p>A chunk is a subset of a region&#39;s fragments, serialized independently. A chunk carries:</p><ul><li>Its fragment data (serde-serializable, per ADR-0044).</li><li>Its dependencies: references to other chunks (by <code>CacheKey</code>, per ADR-0065) that must be loaded before this chunk can be typechecked or eval&#39;d.</li><li>Its <code>replaces</code> targets (so the override graph can be resolved across chunks).</li></ul><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>Chunk = {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    id: ChunkId,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    fragments: FragmentSet,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    dependencies: Set&lt;ChunkId&gt;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    replaces: Map&lt;FragmentId, ChunkId&gt;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    checksum: Blake3,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Chunks are compressed (zstd, per ADR-0065 §3) when stored. Decompression happens on load.</p><h3 id="_3-lazy-load-on-demand-typecheck" tabindex="-1">3. Lazy load, on-demand typecheck <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-lazy-load-on-demand-typecheck" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Lazy load, on-demand typecheck”">​</a></h3><p>The runtime loads the entry-point chunk (the one containing the fragments needed for the program&#39;s first eval). On load:</p><ol><li>Decompress the chunk.</li><li>Resolve dependencies: load each dependency chunk (recursively, depth-first).</li><li>Merge the loaded chunks&#39; fragments (ADR-0044 §5 merge semantics).</li><li>Typecheck the loaded subset (ADR-0044 §1 <code>AstShapeDef</code> payload schemas, ADR-0054 typecheck engine).</li><li>Eval.</li></ol><p>Unloaded chunks are not in memory. If an eval references a fragment in an unloaded chunk (e.g., a <code>Foreign</code> call to a host function whose capability declaration is in another region), the runtime loads that chunk on demand.</p><h3 id="_4-the-dependency-graph-is-the-arrow-structure-not-an-approximation" tabindex="-1">4. The dependency graph is the arrow <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> structure, not an approximation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-the-dependency-graph-is-the-arrow-structure-not-an-approximation" aria-label="Permalink to “4. The dependency graph is the arrow &gt;&gt;&gt; structure, not an approximation”">​</a></h3><p>This is the key property. Because the kernel is arrow calculus (ADR-0052), the compute graph is statically recoverable from the <code>Language</code> data. The chunking layer reads the <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> composition structure to determine which fragments are needed for a given eval, and loads only those chunks. There is no &quot;load everything and hope&quot; fallback; the dependency graph is precise.</p><p>For <code>app</code> (dynamic arrow invocation, ADR-0052 §2), the callee is a runtime value. The chunking layer cannot statically know which chunk the callee&#39;s arrow lives in. Resolution: <code>app</code> triggers a load of the chunk containing the callee&#39;s <code>Language</code> fragment (the callee carries its chunk id, or the runtime resolves it by name). This is the one case where chunking falls back to a runtime lookup — and it is the same case where the arrow model itself acknowledges dynamic dispatch (ADR-0052 §2).</p><h3 id="_5-chunking-is-transparent-to-engines" tabindex="-1">5. Chunking is transparent to engines <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-chunking-is-transparent-to-engines" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Chunking is transparent to engines”">​</a></h3><p>Engines (tree-walk, bytecode, cranelift) operate on a merged <code>Language</code> value (ADR-0044 §1). Chunking produces that merged value from a subset of chunks; the engine does not know whether the <code>Language</code> was assembled from one chunk or fifty. This preserves ADR-0044&#39;s engine-swap property: swapping Pratt for chumsky does not interact with chunking.</p><h3 id="_6-chunking-is-opt-in-and-configurable" tabindex="-1">6. Chunking is opt-in and configurable <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-chunking-is-opt-in-and-configurable" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Chunking is opt-in and configurable”">​</a></h3><p>A runtime config specifies whether chunking is enabled and the region/chunk parameters:</p><div class="language-toml"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">[</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">chunking</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">enabled </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;"> true</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">region_size_target </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">16KB</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">   #</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> merge small regions up to this size</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">chunk_compression </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">zstd:3</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>For compile-time-embedded kits (ADR-0044 §6), chunking is off — the whole <code>Language</code> is in the binary. For runtime-loaded languages, chunking is on when a driver (memory constraint, network load, large definition) is present.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><h3 id="positive" tabindex="-1">Positive <a class="header-anchor" href="#positive" aria-label="Permalink to “Positive”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Memory-bounded language loading.</strong> Only the chunks needed for the current eval are in memory. This makes large <code>Language</code> values (rich standard libraries, multi-kit systems) practical on constrained targets.</li><li><strong>Precise dependency loading, not approximate.</strong> The arrow <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> graph gives exact dependencies. No over-loading, no under-loading.</li><li><strong>Composable with caching (ADR-0065).</strong> Chunks are cacheable units; the cache key (ADR-0065 §2) applies per-chunk, not per-whole-<code>Language</code>. A change in one chunk invalidates only that chunk&#39;s cache entry.</li><li><strong>Transparent to engines.</strong> No engine changes; chunking produces the same merged <code>Language</code> the engines expect.</li></ul><h3 id="negative" tabindex="-1">Negative <a class="header-anchor" href="#negative" aria-label="Permalink to “Negative”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Infrastructure overhead.</strong> Region partitioning, chunk serialization, lazy load, on-demand typecheck, reference resolution — this is a subsystem, not a wrapper. The overhead is justified only when a driver is present; at 180KB with one kit, it is not.</li><li><strong><code>app</code> requires runtime chunk resolution.</strong> Dynamic arrow invocation cannot be statically chunked. The runtime must resolve the callee&#39;s chunk at eval time. This is a fallback, not a failure — but it is a case where the precise-dependency property does not hold.</li><li><strong>Cross-chunk <code>replaces</code> is complex.</strong> A fragment in chunk A that <code>replaces</code> a fragment in chunk B requires B to be loaded before A&#39;s override is resolved. The override graph (ADR-0044 §5) spans chunks; the merge must handle partial graphs and load dependencies on demand.</li><li><strong>Typecheck is per-loaded-subset, not whole-<code>Language</code>.</strong> A fragment&#39;s <code>requires</code> (ADR-0044 §7) may be in an unloaded chunk. The typecheck must either load the chunk (defeating laziness) or defer the check (risking a late error). The design chooses: load the chunk. <code>requires</code> is a hard dependency; if it&#39;s not loaded, the fragment cannot be safely used.</li></ul><h3 id="neutral" tabindex="-1">Neutral <a class="header-anchor" href="#neutral" aria-label="Permalink to “Neutral”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Deferred.</strong> This ADR records the design but does not mandate implementation. A concrete driver (multi-kit, embedded target, network-loaded languages, megabyte-scale definitions) must appear before implementation begins.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-phases-deferred" tabindex="-1">Implementation phases (deferred) <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-phases-deferred" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation phases (deferred)”">​</a></h2><h3 id="phase-1-—-region-partitioning" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — Region partitioning <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-region-partitioning" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — Region partitioning”">​</a></h3><p>Implement <code>region_partition(fragments)</code> using SCC + size-target merging. Test: the canonical kit&#39;s fragments partition into regions; cross-region dependencies are correctly identified.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-chunk-serialization-and-lazy-load" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — Chunk serialization and lazy load <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-chunk-serialization-and-lazy-load" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — Chunk serialization and lazy load”">​</a></h3><p>Serialize chunks independently. Implement lazy load with dependency resolution. Test: load an entry-point chunk, pull in dependencies, produce the same merged <code>Language</code> as whole-<code>Language</code> assembly.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-on-demand-typecheck" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — On-demand typecheck <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-on-demand-typecheck" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — On-demand typecheck”">​</a></h3><p>Typecheck the loaded subset. Test: a program that uses only arithmetic loads only the arithmetic region; a program that uses <code>match</code> loads the <code>match</code> region on demand.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-app-chunk-resolution" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — <code>app</code> chunk resolution <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-app-chunk-resolution" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — app chunk resolution”">​</a></h3><p>Implement runtime chunk resolution for dynamic arrow invocation. Test: a program that <code>app</code>s an arrow from an unloaded chunk loads it correctly.</p><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./0052-arrow-calculus-kernel.html">ADR-0052</a> — Arrow Calculus Kernel (<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> gives the static dependency graph; <code>app</code> is the dynamic case)</li><li><a href="./0044-language-as-data-and-runtime-split.html">ADR-0044</a> — Language as Data and Runtime Split (§5 <code>replaces</code> graph; §7 <code>requires</code>, engine selection)</li><li><a href="./0065-derived-artifact-cache-and-compression.html">ADR-0065</a> — Derived-Artifact Cache and Compression (chunks are cacheable units; per-chunk cache keys)</li><li><a href="./0063-scheme-core-completeness.html">ADR-0063</a> — Scheme-Core Completeness (chunking operates on a complete <code>Language</code>; incomplete cores produce chunks with unresolved <code>Foreign</code> dependencies)</li><li><a href="./0054-generic-typecheck-engine.html">ADR-0054</a> — Generic Typecheck Engine (per-chunk typecheck uses the same engine)</li><li>Game engine chunking (e.g., Unreal Engine World Partition, Minecraft chunk streaming) — the streaming/load-on-demand technique this ADR adapts</li></ul>`,61)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};